<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">James River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The James River is a river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, flowing about 560 kilometres from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appalachian-mountains">Appalachian Mountains</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chesapeake-bay">Chesapeake Bay</a> at Hampton Roads. Named after King James I, it was the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamestown">Jamestown</a>, the first permanent English settlement in America, founded in 1607. Its banks became the heart of Virginia's tobacco plantation economy, and Richmond grew at its falls.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-river.jpg" alt="James River" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thea Ganoe · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JamesRiverWG.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The James River begins in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appalachian-mountains">Appalachian Mountains</a>, at the confluence of the Cowpasture and Jackson Rivers in Botetourt County, and flows 348 miles (560 km) to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chesapeake-bay">Chesapeake Bay</a>; counting the Jackson River it is 444 miles (715 km) long. It is the longest river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, and its catchment covers 10,432 square miles (27,020 km2). Tidal waters extend west to Richmond at the fall line, the head of navigation, where the coastal plain gives way to the piedmont. At its mouth the Elizabeth and Nansemond Rivers join it to form the harbour of Hampton Roads.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Native Americans east of the fall line called the river the Powhatan River, after the Powhatans. The colonists who arrived in 1607 named it after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i">James I of England</a> and built the first permanent English settlement in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> about 35 miles (56 km) upstream from the bay. For its first fifteen years the river was the major highway of colonial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>. In 1612 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-rolfe">John Rolfe</a> cultivated a non-native strain of tobacco that proved popular in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and the river became the main route for exporting it from plantations with wharfs along its banks, which made the Virginia Company's efforts financially successful.</p><p>The James River and Kanawha Canal was meant to link the river to the Ohio Valley through the Kanawha River, but railroads eclipsed canals, and the canal stopped at Eagle Rock; the Chesapeake and Ohio Railway reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio-river">Ohio River</a> by 1873. In the 1960s and 1970s mishandling of the insecticide Kepone contaminated large stretches of the estuary downstream of plants in Hopewell. In December 1975 Governor Mills Godwin Jr. closed 100 miles of the river to fishing, and the ban lasted 13 years. The Chesapeake Bay Agreement of 1983 set baseline protections for the river's clean-up.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamestown">Jamestown</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/williamsburg-virginia">Williamsburg, Virginia</a>'s first colonial capitals, and Richmond, its present capital, lie on the river. It is a major source of drinking water, and a two-mile stretch of rapids ends in downtown Richmond where the river goes over the fall line. The James River Reserve Fleet, an anchorage of mothballed ships of the National Defense Reserve Fleet, lies on its lower course.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “James River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its maker was Wahunsenacah, known to the English as Powhatan after his home town near the falls of the <b>James River</b>, where Richmond now stands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/powhatan-confederacy">Powhatan Confederacy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Warriors under Opechancanough struck English settlements along the <b>James River</b>, killing about a quarter of the colony's population.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-22">22 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Settlers of the Virginia Company came ashore on a peninsula in the <b>James River</b> and built a fort.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-14">14 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Virginia Company of London sent settlers to Jamestown on the <b>James River</b> in 1607, in the heart of the Powhatan Confederacy.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-virginia">Colony of Virginia</a></li></ul></section>
